export default "Period pain shows up in a lot of places. Lower belly. Back. Hip. Leg. Bladder area. Bowel. Rectum. Sometimes during or after sex.\n\nUse this diary as one home for all of it. Pick the sections that match your symptoms. Skip the rest.\n\nIt is not medical advice. It does not diagnose the cause. It does not tell you what care to choose.\n\n## Prompt-care check\n\nGet care now if any of these fit, no matter which section you were about to fill out.\n\n- [ ] Pain is sudden, severe, or getting worse.\n- [ ] Pain is very different from your usual pattern.\n- [ ] Fever.\n- [ ] Vomiting, fainting, or dizziness.\n- [ ] Heavy bleeding.\n- [ ] Blood in urine or black stool.\n- [ ] Pregnancy concern.\n- [ ] New numbness or weakness.\n- [ ] Symptoms that feel unsafe.\n\nIf you feel very sick, seek urgent care.\n\n## Quick-start note\n\nFill this in first. Use plain words.\n\n| Question | Your note |\n|---|---|\n| Date pain started | |\n| Period or cycle day | |\n| Main pain location | |\n| Worst pain, 0 to 10 | |\n| Pain started before flow, with flow, or after flow | |\n| What came with it | |\n| What changed in your day | |\n| Medicine or comfort step already used | |\n| Main question for care | |\n\nMedlinePlus says painful periods can include crampy lower belly pain, back pain, leg pain, nausea, vomiting, loose stools, and headache. ACOG describes dysmenorrhea as pain that comes before or during a period. Those facts do not name your cause. They help you use clear words.\n\n## Daily pain log\n\nUse one row per day.\n\n| Date | Period day | Pain location | Pain 0 to 10 | Cramps | Nausea | Bowel or bladder note | Daily impact |\n|---|---:|---|---:|---|---|---|---|\n| | | lower belly / back / hip / leg / bladder area / rectal area / other | | none / mild / strong | no / yes | | |\n| | | | | | | | |\n| | | | | | | | |\n| | | | | | | | |\n| | | | | | | | |\n| | | | | | | | |\n\n## Lower back pain\n\nUse this if pain settles in your lower back before, during, or after bleeding.\n\n| Date | Period day | Flow | Back pain side | Pain 0 to 10 | Cramps too | Nausea, diarrhea, or headache | Daily impact |\n|---|---|---|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| | day 1 | light / medium / heavy | left / middle / right / both | | no / yes | | |\n| | day 2 | | | | | | |\n| | day 3 | | | | | | |\n\nMedlinePlus lists lower back pain, nausea, diarrhea, and headaches as symptoms that can come with period pain.\n\n## Hip pain\n\nUse this if pain sits in the front hip, side hip, groin, or buttock.\n\n| Area | Left | Right | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Front hip | | | |\n| Side hip | | | |\n| Groin | | | |\n| Buttock | | | |\n| Low back | | | |\n\n| Date | Period day | Side | Hip spot | Pain 0 to 10 | Moved to leg | Walking harder | Sleep changed |\n|---|---:|---|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| | | left / right / both | | | yes / no | yes / no | yes / no |\n| | | | | | | | |\n\n## Leg pain and pain that travels down the leg\n\nUse this if pain runs from the low back, hip, or pelvis into the thigh, knee, calf, or foot.\n\n| Body area | Left | Right | Both | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Low back | | | | |\n| Hip | | | | |\n| Front thigh | | | | |\n| Back thigh | | | | |\n| Knee | | | | |\n| Calf | | | | |\n| Ankle or foot | | | | |\n\n| Date | Period day | Side | Start point | End point | Pain 0 to 10 | Numbness or weakness | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---:|---|---|\n| | | left / right / both | | | | yes / no | |\n| | | | | | | | |\n\nIf you notice numbness or weakness, write down only the facts: area, whether it happened, and what changed from usual. Do not use this table to guess a cause.\n\n## Bladder pain and pressure before or during a period\n\nUse this if pelvic pressure, urgency, frequency, or pee burning shows up around your cycle.\n\n| Date | Cycle day | Bladder pain 0 to 10 | Pelvic pressure | Urgency | Frequency | Pee burn | Blood in urine | Daily impact |\n|---|---:|---:|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| | | | none / mild / strong | no / yes | usual / more / a lot | no / yes | no / yes / not sure | |\n| | | | | | | | | |\n\nNIDDK says to see a health care professional for bladder symptoms such as trouble urinating, pelvic pain, or leaking urine, and to seek care right away for blood in urine or infection symptoms. This table cannot diagnose a UTI, bladder condition, endometriosis, fibroids, or a pregnancy issue.\n\n## Painful bowel movements and rectal pain\n\nUse this if pooping hurts on period days, or if you feel rectal pressure or pain.\n\n| Date | Period day | Bowel pain 0 to 10 | Pelvic pain 0 to 10 | Stool type | Bleeding concern | Notes |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---|---|---|\n| | | | | loose / hard / usual | yes / no | |\n| | | | | | | |\n\nOWH lists painful bowel movements and pain with urination during periods among possible endometriosis symptoms. ACOG says bowel involvement with endometriosis can include pain during bowel movements. These facts do not prove your cause.\n\n**Seek care promptly** for severe or worsening pain, fever, vomiting, blood or black stool, rectal bleeding, blood in urine, dehydration signs, pregnancy concern, or symptoms that feel unsafe.\n\n## Pain during or after sex\n\nUse this if pain shows up with or after sex.\n\n| Date | Cycle day | Pain location | When pain started | Pain 0 to 10 | Duration | Bleeding | Discharge change | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---:|---|---|---|---|\n| | | entry / deep / both | during / after / later | | | none / spotting / light / heavy | yes / no | |\n| | | | | | | | | |\n\nMayo Clinic says care teams may ask when pain began, where it hurts, and how it feels. ACOG and OWH note that pain during or after sex can be part of the endometriosis symptom picture, but pain alone does not prove a diagnosis. Seek prompt care for severe or worsening pelvic pain, heavy bleeding, fever, faintness, dizziness, shoulder pain with pelvic pain, or a pregnancy concern.\n\n## Breast pain after a period\n\nUse this if breast pain, tenderness, or a lump concern shows up.\n\n| Date | Cycle day | Days after period | Side | Spot | Score 0 to 10 | Pain word | Lump concern | Skin change | Nipple discharge |\n|---|---:|---:|---|---|---:|---|---|---|---|\n| | | | left / right / both | one spot / spread out | | sore / sharp / burning / tight | no / yes / not sure | no / yes | no / yes |\n| | | | | | | | | | |\n\nMayo Clinic says breast pain may be cyclic or noncyclic and advises an appointment for pain that continues daily for more than a couple of weeks, stays in one area, worsens, or affects daily activities.\n\n## IUD bleeding and cramps\n\nUse this if cramping and bleeding changed after an IUD insertion, or if a pattern changed months or years later.\n\n| Date | Bleeding | Flow | Cramps | Pain 0 to 10 | Fever note | Odor note | Strings note |\n|---|---|---|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| | none / spotting / bleeding | light / medium / heavy | none / mild / strong | | yes / no | yes / no | checked / not checked / not told to check |\n| | | | | | | | |\n\nACOG says IUDs can affect bleeding patterns and that users should talk with an ob-gyn about questions or problems. CDC recommendations note that bleeding irregularities can happen with IUD use.\n\n## Menstrual cup or tampon pain\n\nUse this if pain, pressure, dryness, or burning shows up with a period product.\n\n| Date | Bleeding day | Product | Pain 0 to 10 | Pressure or dryness | Irritation | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| | before / day 1 / day 2 / day 3+ / after | cup / tampon | | none / mild / strong | none / mild / strong | |\n| | | | | | | |\n\nFDA and MedlinePlus note toxic shock syndrome as a rare but serious tampon-related risk. Seek medical help now for sudden fever, vomiting, diarrhea, fainting or near-fainting, dizziness, or a sunburn-like rash during or soon after a period. ACOG and Cleveland Clinic note that vulvar pain, burning, and irritation can need a clinician visit and possible exam.\n\n## Cramps but no period yet\n\nUse this if cramps start before bleeding, or bleeding has not started at all.\n\n| Date | Cycle day, if known | Pain score | Bleeding or spotting | Other notes |\n|---|---:|---:|---|---|\n| | | | none / spotting / bleeding | |\n| | | | | |\n\nACOG says primary dysmenorrhea can start before or during a period. This table does not interpret pregnancy, miscarriage, or test results.\n\n## Night cramps and sleep impact\n\nUse this if cramps wake you up.\n\n| Date | Period timing | Wake-up time | Pain place | Pain score | Bleeding | Next-day impact |\n|---|---|---|---|---:|---|---|\n| | before bleeding / day 1 / day 2 / after bleeding | | | | none / spotting / light / medium / heavy | |\n| | | | | | | |\n\n## Position notes\n\nUse this if pain feels different lying down, sitting, or in a certain position.\n\n| Date | Period timing | Position | Pain place | Pain before | Pain after | Sleep impact |\n|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---|\n| | before bleeding / day 1 / day 2 / after bleeding | side / back / knees bent / sitting / standing | | | | woke up / no change |\n| | | | | | | |\n\n## Heat and other comfort steps\n\nMedlinePlus and ACOG name heat on the lower belly, exercise, hot baths, rest, and relaxation as things people may try for period pain. FDA notes that over-the-counter pain medicines are used in menstrual cramp products and that people should track pain, what it feels like, and where it hurts.\n\n| Date | Body area | Heat source | Time used | Pain before | Pain after | Skin check |\n|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---|\n| | lower belly / low back / hip / other | heating pad / hot water bottle / wrap / bath | | | | no change / red / sore / numb |\n| | | | | | | |\n\nThis table is a record, not a plan. FDA also warns that pain medicines can cause serious harm when misused, and that NSAIDs should be avoided at 20 weeks or later in pregnancy unless a health care professional specifically advises it.\n\n## Medicine already used\n\n| Product | Active ingredient | Date and time used | Label followed | Helped | Side effects or worries |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| | | | yes / no / not sure | yes / no / not sure | |\n| | | | | | |\n\nDo not use this table to decide a new dose or medicine. Ask a clinician or pharmacist how a label applies to you.\n\n## Cramps around exercise\n\nUse this if cramps show up during or after a workout.\n\n| Date | Period day | Movement type | Timing of cramps | Pain location | Bleeding note | Dizziness or fatigue |\n|---|---:|---|---|---|---|---|\n| | | run / walk / lift / yoga / class / bike / sport | during / after / later / overnight / next day | | light / medium / heavy / changed | dizzy / tired / both / neither |\n| | | | | | | |\n\nCDC describes physical activity broadly and notes that hot conditions can cause muscle cramping, dizziness, and other overheating symptoms alongside period symptoms. This table does not prove that exercise caused your cramps.\n\n## Cramps at work or school\n\n| Date | Work or school block | Pain score | Bathroom access | Product or clothes need | Task impact |\n|---|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| | shift / meeting / class / commute | | easy / hard / none near me | | missed time / slow work / left early / none |\n| | | | | | |\n\nTeens can use a low-detail school script such as \"I have period cramps today. I need to use the bathroom\" or \"I may need a short break today. Please tell me what note or form you need.\" This page does not give legal accommodation advice.\n\n## Asking a support person for help\n\nUse a short note, not your full diary, when you ask someone for help.\n\n```text\nI may need help on period pain days.\nPain often shows up around ________.\nWhat helps: ________\nWhat does not help: ________\nPlease do not share this note.\n```\n\n## One-page visit summary\n\nBring this instead of the full diary.\n\n```text\nPeriod pain and cramp summary\n\nDate range:\nLast period start date:\nWorst pain day and score:\nMain pain location(s):\nWhat came with the pain:\nDaily impact:\nMedicine or comfort steps already used:\nMain question:\n```\n\nMayo Clinic appointment prep for menstrual cramps asks people to write down period dates and cramp severity. Mayo Clinic also says to see a health care professional if menstrual cramps disrupt life every month, symptoms get worse, or severe cramps start after age 25.\n\n## Questions to bring\n\nPick the ones that fit.\n\n- Could my cycle timing matter for this pain?\n- What other causes should we consider?\n- Does the location or pattern change what you check?\n- Should I keep tracking for more than one cycle?\n- What symptoms mean I should call sooner?\n- Should I bring the full log or a short summary?\n- What details can I leave out of an export?\n\n## Privacy note\n\nThis diary can include period dates, sex notes, bowel and bladder details, medicine names, work and school impact, and clinic plans. Keep only what helps your care question.\n\nFloriva can keep cycle and pain notes on your device. Paper works too. HHS notes that HIPAA applies to covered entities and business associates, not every consumer app in every use, and the FTC's Health Breach Notification Rule can apply to some health apps when covered health information is breached. Screenshots, exports, backups, portals, and shared devices sit outside any app's control.\n\nFor visit prep, read [Floriva for gynecologist prep](/app-guides/floriva-for-gynecologist-prep).";
